	<p> Match up the elements of <m>\mathbb{Z}_{2} \oplus \mathbb{Z}_{3}</m> with the elements of <m>\mathbb{Z}_{6} </m>.</p>

     <p>Hint: Match up identities, units, and zero divisors, and use
     <m>(1,1)+(1,1)=(0,2)</m> and <m>1+1 = 2</m>.</p>

     
     <sidebyside>
  		<p><m>(0,0) \to</m> <fillin /></p>
		<p><m>(0,1) \to</m> <fillin /></p>
		<p><m>(0,2) \to</m> <fillin /></p>
		<p><m>(1,0) \to</m> <fillin /></p>
		<p><m>(1,1) \to</m> <fillin /></p>
		<p><m>(1,2) \to</m> <fillin /></p>
     </sidebyside>

	<p>Write new copies of both <m>\Z_{2}\oplus \Z_{3}</m> tables, but replace each element with your matched up element of <m>\Z_{6}</m>.</p>
	
	
    </page>


	<page>
	
	<paragraphs>
		<title>Isomorphism definition</title>
    <p>
    What we want is for the addition and multiplication tables to <q>match up</q>
    (up to a reordering).
    Of course, writing this all down and redrawing addition and multiplication
    tables would be tedious, and impossible for infinite groups.
    So, consider that in our <m>\mathbb{Z}_{2}\oplus\mathbb{Z}_{3}</m> addition
    table we had:
    <md>
        (1,2) + (0,1) = (1,0)
    </md>
    and we said
    <md>
        (1,2)\to [5], \,\,\,(0,1)\to [4], \,\,\, \text{ and }\, \,\, (1,0)\to [3].
    </md>
    So the header column of this entry gets replaced with <m>[5]</m>, the header
    row with <m>[4]</m> and the entry with <m>[3]</m>.
    For this to match the <m>\mathbb{Z}_{6}</m> addition table, we need
    <md>
        [5]+[4] = [3],
    </md>
    which it does!
    Calling our map or function <m>\phi</m> and writing
    <m>\phi((1,2))= [5], \phi((0,1))= [4],</m> and <m>\phi((1,0))= [3]</m>, what
    we need is
    <md>
        \phi((1,2)) + \phi((0,1))=[5]+[4] =[3]= \phi((1,0))=\phi((1,2)+(0,1))
    </md>
    We call this idea, that <m>\phi(a)+\phi(b) = \phi(a+b)</m>,
    <em>preserving addition</em>.
</p>

<definition>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>R</m> and <m>S</m> be rings.
            An <term>isomorphism</term> is a bijective function
            <m>\phi: R \to S</m> such that for all <m>x,y\in R</m>,
            <md>
                    <mrow>\phi(x+y)=\phi(x) + \phi(y) \text{ and }</mrow>
                    <mrow>\phi(xy) = \phi(x)\phi(y)</mrow>.
            </md>
            If there exists an isomorphism from <m>R</m> to <m>S</m>, then
            <m>R</m> is said to be <term>isomorphic</term> to <m>S</m>, denoted <m>R \cong M</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
</definition>
    </paragraphs>
    
    <paragraphs>
    	<title>Proving isomorphism</title>
	
		<theorem>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let
            <m>\mathcal{M}= \left\{\begin{bmatrix}a &amp;-b \\ b &amp;a\end{bmatrix} : a,b\in\mathbb{R}\right\}</m>.
            Then <m>\mathcal{M}</m> is a ring and
            <m>\mathcal{M}\cong \mathbb{C}</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
</theorem>

<exercise workspace="1in">
<p>
    We'll do the isomorphism part first.
    Come up with a reasonable function <m>\phi</m> that maps elements of
    <m>\mathcal{M}</m> to elements of <m>\mathbb{C}</m>.
</p>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1in">
<p>
    Show <m>\phi</m> is an injection by showing that for two matrices <m>X</m>
    and <m>Y</m> in <m>\mathcal{M}</m>, if <m>\phi(X) =\phi(Y)</m> then
    <m>X = Y</m>.
</p>
</exercise>
</paragraphs>
</page>

<page>
<exercise workspace="1.5in">
<p>
    Show <m>\phi</m> is a surjection by showing that for an arbitrary
    <m>x+yi\in \mathbb{C}</m>, there exists an element of <m>\mathcal{M}</m>
    that maps to it.
</p>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1.5in">
<p>
    Show <m>\phi</m> preserves addition, that is, show for all
    <m>X,Y\in \mathcal{M}</m>, <m>\phi(X+Y) = \phi(X)+\phi(Y)</m>.
</p>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1.5in">
<p>
    Show <m>\phi</m> preserves multiplication, that is, show
    <m>\phi(XY) = \phi(X)\phi(Y)</m>.
</p>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1.5in">
<p>
    This means <m>M \cong \mathbb{C}</m>.
    <em>If time:</em> Since <m>\mathbb{C}</m> is a field, this will actually
    imply that <m>M</m> Is a field.
    So every nonzero element of <m>M</m> is a unit.
    Can you find the inverse for an arbitrary matrix?
</p>
</exercise>

	</page>
	
<page>
<paragraphs>
	<title>Invariants</title>
	
	 <definition>
     	<statement>
		<p>An <term>invariant of ring isomorphism</term> is a property <m>P</m> such that
     if <m>R</m> satisfies <m>P</m> and <m>R\cong S</m> then <m>S</m> satisfies
     <m>P</m>.</p>
     	</statement>
	</definition>

 <p>
     Let's prove that having a multiplicative identity is an invariant of rings. That is:
 </p>

 <theorem>
     <statement>
         <p>
             If <m>R</m> and <m>S</m> are rings, if <m>R</m> has a multiplicative
             identity and <m>R\cong S</m>, then <m>S</m> has a multiplicative
             identity.
         </p>
     </statement>
 </theorem>

<exercise workspace="1in">
 <p>
     We'll do a direct proof.
     What should you assume?
 </p>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1in">
 <p>
     One thing you are assuming is that <m>R</m> has a multiplicative identity.
     Write what you can conclude using the definition of multiplicative identity.
 </p>
 </exercise>

<exercise workspace="1in">
 <p>
     One thing you are assuming is that <m>R\cong S</m>.
     Write what you can conclude using the definition of isomorphism.
 </p>
 </exercise>

<exercise workspace="1in">
 <p>
     How can you rewrite <m>\phi(1_{R} \cdot x)</m> and <m>\phi(x\cdot 1_{R}</m>
     ) using the definition of isomorphism?
 </p>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1in">
 <p>
     How can you rewrite <m>\phi(1_{R}\cdot x)</m> and <m>\phi(x\cdot 1_{R})</m>
     using <m>1_{R}\cdot x = x = x\cdot 1_{R}</m>?
 </p>
 </exercise>

<exercise workspace="1in">
 <p>
     Let <m>y\in S</m>.
     Use that <m>\phi</m> is a surjection and your work above to show that for
     all <m>y\in S</m>, <m>\phi(1_{R})\cdot y = y = y\cdot \phi(1_{R})</m>.
 </p>
</exercise>

</paragraphs>
</page>


<page>

<exercise workspace="3in">
 <p>
     Which of the following do you think are invariants of ring isomorphism?
     <ul>
         <li>
             <p>
                 Number of elements
             </p>
         </li>

         <li>
             <p>
                 Commutativity
             </p>
         </li>

         <li>
             <p>
                 The existence of an identity
             </p>
         </li>

         <li>
             <p>
                 The existence or number of zero divisors
             </p>
         </li>

         <li>
             <p>
                 The existence or number of units (in a ring with identity)
             </p>
         </li>

         <li>
             <p>
                 Being a field
             </p>
         </li>

         <li>
             <p>
                 Being an integral domain
             </p>
         </li>
     </ul>
 </p>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="3in">
 <p>
     Spoiler alert: they are all invariants!
 </p>
 <p>
     Show that commutativity is an invariant and use it to conclude that
     <m>\mathcal{M}_{2\times 2}(\mathbb{Z})</m> is not isomorphic to
     <m>\mathbb{Z}\oplus \mathbb{Z}\oplus \mathbb{Z}\oplus \mathbb{Z}</m>.
 </p>
 </exercise>

</page>

<page>
<paragraphs>
	<title>Disproving isomorphism</title>
	
	<p>
    Turns out rings can have lots of things in common, e.g., both being fields,
    and yet not be isomorphic.
    For example, <m>\mathbb{R}</m> and <m>\mathbb{C}</m> are both infinitely large fields, but
    they are not isomorphic.
</p>


<p>
    Suppose that <m>\phi: \mathbb{C}\to \mathbb{R}</m> is an isomorphism.
</p>

<exercise workspace="1.5in">
<p>
    Show that <m>\phi(0_{\mathbb{C}}) =0_{\mathbb{R}}</m>.
    (Hint: it suffices to show that <m>x + \phi(0_{\mathbb{C}}) = x</m> for all
    <m>x\in \mathbb{R}</m>.
    You may need to use that fact that <m>\phi</m> is surjective.)
</p>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1.5in">
<p>
    Using a similar argument as above, show that
    <m>\phi(1_{\mathbb{C}}) = 1_{\mathbb{R}}</m>.
</p>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1.5in">
<p>
    Use the definition of isomorphism and your answers to the previous parts to
    argue that <m>\phi(i)</m> is an element of <m>\mathbb{R}</m> and
    <m>\phi(i)^{2} = -1</m>.
    (Hint: Apply <m>\phi</m> to both sides of the equation <m>i^{2}+1=0</m>.)
</p>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1.5in">
<p>
    Explain why the result you proved in the previous part is a contradiction.
    Deduce that <m>\mathbb{C}</m> cannot be isomorphic to <m>\mathbb{R}</m>.
</p>
</exercise>

</paragraphs>
</page>




<page>
<paragraphs>
<title>Review questions</title>
Make sure you can answer the following questions:

<ul>
	<li><p>What is the definition of ring isomorphism? </p></li>
	<li><p>How do you prove two rings are isomorphic?</p></li>
	<li><p>What are some examples of invariants?</p></li>
	<li><p>Can you use an invariant to prove two rings are isomorphic? Can you use an invariant to prove two rings are not isomorphic?</p></li>
</ul>
                
<p>The rest of this page is for you to summarize important ideas from the module.</p>

</paragraphs>

</page>


<page>

<paragraphs>
    <title>Weekly Practice 9</title>

		<exercise number="1">
		<title>The number of zero divisors in a ring is an invariant</title>
		<introduction><p>
    Prove that the number of zero divisors in a ring is an invariant under
    isomorphism by filling in the blanks below.
		</p>		
		</introduction>
		
		<task>
		<p>We will use a lemma, which we will assume without proof.</p>
		<lemma>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>R</m> and <m>S</m> be isomorphic rings.
            Let <m>0_{R}</m> be the additive identity of <m>R</m> and
            <m>0_{S}</m> be the additive identity of <m>S</m>.
            For any isomorphism <m>\phi: R\to S</m>, <m>\phi(0_{R}) = 0_{S}</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
</lemma>

<theorem>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>R</m> and <m>S</m> be isomorphic rings and let <m>R_{0}</m>
            and <m>S_{0}</m> be the subset of zero divisors of <m>R</m> and
            <m>S</m>, respectively.
            Then <m>|R_{0}| = |S_{0}|</m> and hence, the number of zero divisors
            is an invariant of ring isomorphism.
        </p>
    </statement>
</theorem>

<proof>
    <p>
        Let <m>R</m> and <m>S</m> be isomorphic rings.
        Thus, there exists an isomorphism <m>\phi: R\to S</m>.
        This means <m>\phi</m> <fillin fill="Lots of stuff" />
    </p>

    <p>
        We will show that if <m>x\in R_{0}</m> then <m>\phi(x)\in S_{0}</m>.
        That is, we will show that if <m>x\in R</m> is a zero divisor in
        <m>R</m> then <m>\phi(x)</m> is <fillin fill="a zero divisor in S" />.
        Since <m>x</m> is a zero divisor in <m>R</m>, <m>x\neq 0_{R}</m> and
        there exists a <m>y\in R</m> such that <fillin /> and <m>xy = 0_{R}</m> or
        <m>yx=0_{R}</m>.
        Without loss of generality, suppose <m>xy = 0_{R}</m>.
        Then
    </p>
    <md>
        \underline{\hspace{0.5in}}= \phi(0_{R}) = \phi(xy) = \underline{\hspace{.5in}}.
    </md>

    <p>
        By the lemma <m>\phi(x) \neq</m> <fillin /> and <m>\phi(y) \neq</m> <fillin />.
        Thus <m>\phi(x)</m> is a <fillin />.
        Since <m>\phi</m> is a bijection, <m>|R_{0}|= |S_{0}|</m>.
    </p>
</proof>
		</task>
		
		<task>
		<p>
    Use this to prove that <m>\mathbb{Z}_{4}</m> is not isomorphic to
    <m>\mathbb{Z}_{2} \oplus \mathbb{Z}_{2}</m>.
		</p>
		</task>
    	</exercise>
	</paragraphs>
	</page>
	
	<page>
	
		<exercise number="2">
		<title>A matrix isomorphism</title>
		<introduction><p>
    Let
    <m>M = \left\{ \begin{bmatrix}x &amp;0\\ -x &amp;0\end{bmatrix}: x\in\mathbb{R}\right\}</m>.
    Let <m>\phi: M \to \mathbb{R}</m> be defined by
    <md>
        \phi\left( \begin{bmatrix}x &amp;0\\ -x &amp;0\end{bmatrix} \right) = x.
    </md>
    This problem will show <m>\phi</m> is an isomorphism and thus <m>M</m> and
    <m>\mathbb{R}</m> are isomorphic.
</p></introduction>
        	<task><p>
    Show <m>\phi</m> is an injection by showing for <m>A,B\in M</m> if
    <m>\phi(A) = \phi(B)</m> then <m>A = B</m>.
    Then show <m>\phi</m> is a surjection by showing for an arbitrary
    <m>a\in \mathbb{R}</m> there exists <m>A\in M</m> such that
    <m>\phi(A) = a</m>.
</p></task>
            <task><p>
    Show <m>\phi</m> preserves addition by showing for all <m>A,B \in M</m>,
    <m>\phi(A+B) = \phi(A) + \phi(B)</m>.
</p></task>

			<task><p>
    Show <m>\phi</m> preserves multiplication by showing for all
    <m>A,B\in M</m>, <m>\phi(A\cdot B) = \phi(A)\cdot \phi(B)</m>.
</p></task>
			
			<task><p>
    The above problems show that <m>\phi</m> is an isomorphism and hence
    <m>M</m> and <m>\mathbb{R}</m> are isomorphic.
    Since being a field is an invariant and <m>\mathbb{R}</m> is a field, we can
    conclude <m>M</m> is a field.
    Thus, every nonzero element of <m>M</m> is a unit.
    What is the identity of <m>M</m>?
    Find the multiplicative inverse of
    <m>A = \begin{bmatrix}a &amp;0 \\ -a &amp;0\end{bmatrix}</m>.
</p></task>
		</exercise>
		
		<exercise number="3">
		<title>Homomorphisms: Kind of like isomorphisms, but not</title>
		<introduction><p>
    Let <m>R</m> and <m>S</m> be rings.
    A function <m>\phi: R\to S</m> is a <term>homomorphism</term> of rings if
    <m>\phi(a+b) =\phi(a)+\phi(b)</m> and <m>\phi(ab)=\phi(a)\phi(b)</m> for all
    <m>a,b\in \mathbb{R}</m>. (Notice how this is very similar to the definition of <em>isomorphism</em>, but something important is missing -- which requirement of an isomorphism is <em>not</em> required here?)
</p></introduction>
		
			<task><p>
    Define <m>\phi: \mathbb{Z}\to \mathbb{Z}_{5}</m> by
    <m>\phi(k) = [k]_{5}</m>.
    Show that <m>\phi</m> is a homomorphism.
</p></task>

			<task><p>
    The <term>kernel</term> of a homomorphism <m>\phi</m> is the set
    <m>\text{Ker}(\phi) = \{ r\in R: \phi(r) = 0_{S}\}</m>.
    Find <m>\text{Ker}(\phi)</m> for the <m>\phi</m> in part (a).
</p></task>
			
			<task><p>
    Prove the kernel you found in part b is an ideal of <m>\mathbb{Z}</m>.
    (It turns out the kernel of a ring homomorphism is always an ideal.)
</p></task>
		</exercise>
	
 </page>
 
